; live-inquiry-band.fk — state derived from live readings, questions composed from blocks.
;
; preludes: form-stdlib/core.fk observe/live-inquiry.fk
;
; The "now" fixture is the phone as it actually sat at 2026-07-23 00:32: 14 milli-m/s2 of
; residual motion, 1374 millidegrees off flat, ordinal brightness 3.
;
; Verdict 511 when, on Go / Rust / TypeScript / fkwu:
;     1  14 mm/s2, flat, lit -> still
;     2  900 mm/s2 -> moving, whatever else is true
;     4  flat-ness lost past 15 degrees -> held
;     8  brightness 1 in a still flat phone -> dark
;    16  and the phone right now reads still
;    32  which composes four questions
;    64  the first being the barometer's ambiguity, dissolved by a second sensor
;   128  while moving composes two, and not the same two
;   256  seven compositions exist and no single state offers all seven
;
; The teaching is bit 64. A barometer alone cannot tell weather from stairs — the reading is
; identical. Composed with stillness the ambiguity resolves in BOTH directions at once: a
; still phone's pressure change is the weather, a moving phone's is altitude. That is what a
; composed question is for. Not two answers side by side; one answer neither sensor could
; give alone.
;
; And bit 256 is the honest counterweight: the compositions are state-bound, so a body in
; one state can never ask everything it knows how to ask. What can be asked depends on how
; the thing is currently sitting, which is why the state is derived and not declared.

(do
    (live-inquiry-check))
